|> Exercise
    work=ing
    handle=_cosine_rule_geometric_1_

    In the figure below, where can

    $$
    \Large ab\cos\theta_{ab}
    $$

    be found, if at all? (Note that “$\theta_{ab}$” is
    just a name.)

    |> Image
        src=images/e5_cosine_rule_geometric_1_statement.svg

    |> Solution

        It appears two places; 
        it is 
        !! it is, in the first instance, 
        the area of this
        rectangle here on the left:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_cosine_rule_geometric_1_sol1.svg

        It is also
        the area of this rectangle here on the right:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_cosine_rule_geometric_1_sol2.svg

        In more detail, here is how “$b\cdot\cos\theta_{ab}$” 
        and “$a\cdot\cos\theta_{ab}$” 
        show up as the widths of the two rectangles, respectively, 
        zooming in:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_cosine_rule_geometric_1_sol3.svg

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_cosine_rule_geometric_1_sol4.svg

        |> Pause

        This means that the rectangles have areas

        $$
        \Large a(b\cdot \cos\theta_{ab}) = b(a\cdot \cos\theta_{ab}) = ab\cos\theta_{ab}
        $$

        while forming the product in two different ways,
        but arriving at the same end result.